<sn><md>#### Dietary Sources

Lipids are broadly categorized based on where they originate:

+++ view
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/dietary_source_of_lipids.png)
+++

1. **Animal Sources:** Found in fatty `meats, butter, ghee, cheese, and egg yolks`.
2. **Plant Sources:** Derived from `vegetable oils` (such as mustard, soybean, sesame, linseed, maize, coconut, sunflower`, and palm oil) as well as `nuts` (including cashews, pistachios, and peanuts).
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Key Functions of Lipids

+++ view
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/function_of_lipids.png)
+++

* **Energy Production:** Delivers the `highest amount of heat and energy` per gram compared to other macronutrients.
* **Growth and Maintenance:** Provides `structural support` essential for physical growth and systemic nutrition.
* **Energy Storage &amp; Temperature Regulation:** Functions as a `reserve energy source` while preventing thermal heat loss.
* **Dermatological Health:** Maintains `skin hydration, softness`, and structural integrity, protecting against certain `skin conditions`.
* **Nutrient Absorption:** Facilitates the dissolution and biological absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K).
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Health Effects of Lipid Imbalance

+++ ...
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/General%20Science/B.Z.Food%20and%20Nutrition/assets/lipid_imbalance.png)
+++

* **Deficiency Effects:** ++Short-term++ deficiency leads to `dry, rough skin`. 
  ++Extended shortage++ forces the body to `break down stored muscle protein`, resulting in `weight loss and muscle wasting`.
* **Excess Effects:** Overconsumption can `restrict blood circulation` and lead to `obesity`, raising the overall `risk of metabolic and cardiovascular diseases`.</md></sn>